              "rule": "input like {d9b038751caa4c7fa2ee7ca7860de13c}",
              "score": 10
            },
            {
              "feedback": {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Incorrect. Remember that in titration there are generally four stages to consider: the initial point before the titration begins, the portion before the equivalence point is reached, the equivalence point where the number of moles acid and moles of base are equal, and the portion after the equivalence point is passed. The stage at which the moles of analyte (the initial acid or base being titrated) equals the moles of titrant (the base or acid being added to the initial solution) is the equivalence point, which is identified by a mid-range pH and extreme changes in pH around the equivalence point."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "ae9ff6358d34445ba380a9e0261b5c91",
                    "type": "p"
